00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:34.860
in today's briefing so we cannot report wind rate or cumulative alpha. Now here is something

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:40.740
most retail investors get wrong and it cost me money before I figured it out. Imagine you bought

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:48.320
an energy ETF last quarter when oil supply headlines first emerged. Your target allocation was 15%

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:54.840
but drift from price moves has pushed it to 19%. Rebalancing requires calculating current

00:06:54.840 --> 00:07:00.220
weights using end-of-quarter market values then comparing against your written policy ranges.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:06.140
Professionals always check realized versus unrealized gains first to decide whether loss

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:12.600
harvesting in non-registered accounts can offset the rebalance. Export your brokerage CSV.

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:18.820
Some dividend income separately from price appreciation and subtract total fees paid over

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:24.400
the quarter before deciding on new positions. The biggest mistake with quarterly reviews is

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.500
treating them as performance scorecards instead of allocation and tax planning exercises.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:35.820
Run the decision checklist in this order. Asset allocation drift, position sizing limits,

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:43.380
tax loss opportunities, then new capital deployment. Before we wrap check one energy name or ETF

